===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|sadelen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sadolian}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*sadulōną}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To put a saddle on (an animal).
# To get into a saddle.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[burden]] or [[encumber]].
#: {{ux|en|He has been '''saddled''' with the task of collecting evidence of the theft.}}
#: {{ux|en|They went shopping and left me '''saddled''' with two children to look after.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=December|title=Dr. Beeching previews the plan for British Railways|journal=Modern Railways|page=377|text=They '''saddled''' themselves with the handling of light flows on a multiplicity of branch lines, and they sacrificed the speed, reliability and low cost of through train operation, even over the main arteries of the system.}}

